{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothInductionStep","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.RothInductionStep","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SeparatedWronskian","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SeparatedIndex","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SeparatedHeight","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.WronskianIndex","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothRecursionArith"],"declarations":20,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_two_mul_max_of_det_bound_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_two_mul_max_of_det_bound_witness","kind":"theorem","line":612,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L612-L633","doc":"**Satisfiability of the `max` form**, on the same `1 × 1` instance: `θ = 1`, `t = 0`,\n`rr = 1`, `S = 1`, `q = 1`, conclusion `1 ≤ 0 + 2·max 1 1 = 2`.\n\n⚠ Note what this cannot test.  The distinction between this theorem and the superseded\n`index_le_of_det_bound` is asymptotic — the latter's `S·rr ≤ r` is satisfiable at every small\n`r` and only fails in the regime the recursion runs in.  No finite witness separates them;\nthat is why correction 9 needed the parameter check rather than another example. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_or_sq_of_det_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_or_sq_of_det_bound","kind":"theorem","line":581,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L581-L610","doc":"**§13's two branches, unrounded** — the shape display (2) actually has.\n\n`index_le_two_mul_max_of_det_bound` packages the two cases into `t + 2·max(S/r, q)`, and in\ndoing so takes a square root of the second one, losing a factor `√2`\n(`RothArith.sq_le_two_mul_max_of_sum_le`'s docstring).  This keeps them apart and keeps the\nsecond squared, which is what display (2) does:\n\n    J(A) ≤ t + √((2/r_m)·J(W))  ≤  t + 2·√(J(W)/n).\n\nA caller converts the squared branch with `RothLemmaStatement.radicand_weaken` — the source's\nown step, `2/r_m ≤ 4/n` from `n ≤ r_m + 1` — instead of rounding here.\n\n⚠ No `q` and no side condition: the two branches are complementary by construction, so this\nform needs neither. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_two_mul_max_of_det_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_two_mul_max_of_det_bound","kind":"theorem","line":544,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L544-L579","doc":"**Mahler §13, combined — the real form, and it needs no side condition.**\n\n    θ ≤ t + 2 · max ( S/r , q )        for any `q` with `S ≤ q²·rr`.\n\nBoth branches are useful and they are complementary by construction:\n\n* if `(r−1)/rr ≤ θ − t` the cut-off falls outside the matrix, every column contributes, and\n  Gauss's sum eats half of them — `θ − t ≤ 2S/r`;\n* otherwise `(θ−t)·rr < r`, which is exactly the hypothesis of `sq_index_le_of_det_bound`, and\n  that gives `θ − t ≤ √(2S/rr) ≤ 2q`.\n\nThe `1/r` in the first branch is what makes it worth having: in the application `S` is of\nsize `r·Φ`, so `S/r` is of size `Φ`.  Dropping it — which is what `index_le_add_of_det_bound`\ndoes, keeping only the `j = 0` term — gives a bound of size `r·Φ` and is useless.  That was\ncorrection 9.\n\n⚠ Compare `index_le_of_det_bound` above, which reaches `θ ≤ t + 2q` but needs `S·rr ≤ r`.\nThis needs nothing, and is weaker only by the `max` — which costs nothing, since `S/r` is\nitself a good bound. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_det_bound_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_det_bound_witness","kind":"theorem","line":520,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L520-L540","doc":"**Satisfiability of `index_le_of_det_bound`.**  The same `1 × 1` instance as\n`sq_index_le_of_det_bound_witness`, with `S = 1`, `rr = 1`, `r = 1`, `q = 1`: the side\ncondition `S·rr ≤ r` reads `1 ≤ 1` and the conclusion reads `1 ≤ 2`.\n\n⚠ As before, this certifies joint satisfiability only — it does not exercise the truncation,\nand at `q = S = 1` the two branches happen to agree. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_det_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_det_bound","kind":"theorem","line":493,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L493-L518","doc":"⚠ **Superseded by `index_le_two_mul_max_of_det_bound`.**  `θ ≤ t + 2q` whenever\n`q ≥ √(S/rr)` (stated as `S ≤ q²·rr`) and `S·rr ≤ r`.\n\nThe theorem is true.  Its hypothesis `S·rr ≤ r` is the problem: it was introduced to force\n§13's second branch, on the mistaken belief (correction 8) that the first branch was\nworthless, and in the recursion's regime it reads `Φ_m·r_m ≤ 1` and is **false**.  Kept as the\nrecord of correction 9; use the `max` form instead. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.max_form_implied_by_case_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.max_form_implied_by_case_one","kind":"theorem","line":482,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L482-L491","doc":"The vacuous `max` form, derived from the unsharpened case (1) alone.\n\n⚠ This is a record of correction 8, and correction 9 explains what it does **not** show: the\nsource's combined display has `J(W)/n` in the first branch, and *that* form is not derivable\nthis way.  Nothing should use this theorem; it exists so the mis-transcription stays visible\nrather than being quietly deleted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.factor_bound_of_rescaled","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.factor_bound_of_rescaled","kind":"theorem","line":406,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L406-L432","doc":"**The reusable half of `index_le_of_rescaled_factor_bounds`.**\n\nThat theorem's *rescaling* is correct and is what the source's `p_h = n·r_h` does; only the\nlinear conclusion drawn from it was too weak (correction 6).  This isolates the rescaling:\nbounds on the two Wronskians' indices **at the rescaled degrees** give a bound on their sum at\nthe unscaled ones, with the factor `r` the recursion needs.\n\n⚠ The factor `r` in the conclusion is the source's `J(W) ≤ n·Φ_m`, and it is not slack: it\ncancels against the `S/r` in §13's conclusion.  A version without it would be asking the\ninduction hypothesis for something strictly stronger than it can give. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_add_of_det_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_add_of_det_bound","kind":"theorem","line":372,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L372-L404","doc":"**Mahler §13, case (1).**  `θ ≤ t + S`, with no hypothesis relating `θ`, `rr` and `r`.\n\nThe source presents this as the case `n ≤ N`, which invites formalising a hypothesis that is\nnot needed: the bound is just the `j = 0` term of `sum_max_le_index_det`, and every other\nterm is non-negative.  It holds in **every** regime.  (What the case `n ≤ N` really says is\nthat in that regime this is the *best* bound available — a statement about strength, not\nabout validity.)\n\nNeeds `0 < r` only so that column `0` exists; at `r = 0` the determinant is `1` and carries\nno information about `P`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sq_index_le_of_det_bound_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sq_index_le_of_det_bound_witness","kind":"theorem","line":346,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L346-L369","doc":"**Satisfiability of `sq_index_le_of_det_bound`.**  One variable, unit weight, the origin,\n`P = X₀`, a `1 × 1` matrix whose only operator list is empty: `θ = 1`, `t = 0`, `rr = 1`,\n`S = 1`, and the conclusion reads `1 ≤ 2`.\n\n⚠ Read this for what it is.  It certifies that the four hypotheses are **jointly\nsatisfiable** — a stack of side conditions that silently contradict each other would make the\ntheorem vacuous, and this rules that out.  It does **not** exercise the truncation: with\n`r = 1` there is only one column and nothing is clipped.  The guard for the truncation itself\nis `RothArith.sum_max_truncation_active`, where it is cheap to state exactly. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sq_index_le_of_det_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sq_index_le_of_det_bound","kind":"theorem","line":320,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L320-L342","doc":"**The square-root bound.**  With a lower bound `θ` on `index P`, per-column operator costs\nbounded by `t + j/rr`, and an upper bound `S` on the index of the determinant, the excess\n`θ − t` satisfies `(θ − t)² · rr ≤ 2S`.\n\nThe hypothesis `hn : (θ − t)·rr ≤ r` is Mahler's \"the cut-off falls inside the matrix\" — it\nsays the determinant has at least as many columns as the truncation needs.  Without it the\nsum over `Fin r` is missing positive terms and the conclusion is false. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rescaled_factor_bounds","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_le_of_rescaled_factor_bounds","kind":"theorem","line":212,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L212-L286","doc":"**The induction step's inequality with the rescaling applied.**\n\nGiven upper bounds on the two factors' indices *in their own degree vectors* — which is the\nshape the induction hypothesis produces, since `mvGenWronskian` of `r` functions of degree\n`≤ d` has degree `≤ r·d` — and a lower bound `θ` on `index P`, this concludes\n\n    θ ≤ εU + εV + (r/D₁ + r/D₂).\n\nThe rescaling is what makes the `εU`, `εV` terms come out un-divided: without it the induction\nhypothesis simply does not apply to `U` and `V`, because they are not of degree `d`.\n\n═══ ⚠ THIS CONFIRMS THE SIXTH CORRECTION RATHER THAN RESOLVING IT ═══\n\nThe error term is now `r/D₁ + r/D₂`, down a factor `r` from the unscaled\n`sum_index_le_index_sepW_concrete`.  **It is still too large.**\n\n`r` is the length of a minimal separated representation, so it is bounded by the number of\ndistinct monomials in the split-off variable group — of order `D₁`.  Then `r/D₁` is of order\n`1`, and Roth's lemma needs a conclusion of the form `index ≤ m·ε` with `ε` small.  An error\nof order `1` swamps it.  The `r/D₂` term is worse: under the growth condition the two degree\nscales differ by a factor `ω`, so `r/D₂` is of order `ω`.\n\n⚠ Two things to be clear about in that estimate:\n\n* the bound `r ≲ D₁` is **not proved here** — it is a plausible bound on the length of a\n  minimal representation, and the magnitude argument rests on it;\n* the theorem below is **correct** regardless; what is in question is whether it is *strong\n  enough*, which is a statement about constants, not about validity.\n\nSo the honest status is unchanged from the sixth correction: the arc has a proved inequality\nthat is **not established** to yield Roth's lemma, and applying the rescaling did not close\nthat.  Reconstructing the correct constant bookkeeping needs the actual argument, not more\nlemmas — every ingredient here is tight given only \"operators have order at most `r`\", so the\nloss is structural and must be repaired upstream of this statement. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sum_index_le_index_sepW_concrete","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sum_index_le_index_sepW_concrete","kind":"theorem","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L170-L208","doc":"**The central inequality with the cost made concrete:**\n\n    r · index P  ≤  index U + index V + r²·(1/D₁ + 1/D₂).\n\n⚠⚠ **This is where a problem with the previous docstring's claim becomes visible, and it is\nworth stating rather than hiding.**\n\n`r` is the length of a minimal separated representation, so `r ≤ (number of distinct\n`x`-monomials of `P`) ≤ d₁ + 1`.  Then `r²/D₁ ≈ d₁`, and the error term is of the same order\nas the trivial bound on the index itself.  In that regime the inequality is **true but\nuseless**.\n\nThe classical proof does not have this problem, because it measures the Wronskian's index\nagainst the **Wronskian's own** degree vector — `W` has degree `≤ r·d₁` in `x`, so the weights\nused for `index W` are `r·d` rather than `d`.  Every statement in this arc uses one degree\nvector `d` throughout, `index_sepW` included.\n\n⚠ So: this inequality is **proved and correct**, and it is **not established** that it is the\nform Roth's lemma needs.\n\nThe rescaling *tool* now exists — `idxWeight_scale_degree`, `le_index_scale_degree_iff` and\n`index_scale_degree_le_iff` in `QOU/Mathlib/RothIndex.lean`, with scale factor `r` supplied by\n`degreeOf_mvGenWronskian_le`.  ⚠ But having the tool is not the same as having used it:\nnothing below is stated in rescaled weights, and whether the error term actually becomes small\nis the recursion's arithmetic.  Nothing here should be read as \"the index side of the\ninduction step is done\". ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.cost_bound_of_bounded_order","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.cost_bound_of_bounded_order","kind":"theorem","line":153,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L153-L168","doc":"A concrete bound on the per-entry cost, given operator lists of bounded length and a floor\n`D₁`, `D₂` on the two degree vectors. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sum_map_inv_append_sumElim","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sum_map_inv_append_sumElim","kind":"theorem","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L144-L151","doc":"The cost of a mixed operator list splits across the two variable groups. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sum_index_le_index_sepW_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sum_index_le_index_sepW_add","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L103-L140","doc":"**`r · index P ≤ index U + index V + r·C`** — the induction step's central inequality.\n\nRead: differentiating `P` into its separated Wronskian costs at most `C` of index per entry,\nand the Wronskian's index then splits exactly between the two factors.  So a *large* index for\n`P` forces a large index for one of `U`, `V`, which is what the induction hypothesis\ncontradicts.\n\n⚠ **An earlier version of this docstring called this \"the inequality the recursion runs on.\"\nThat was an overclaim** — see `sum_index_le_index_sepW_concrete` below, which makes the cost\nconcrete and thereby exposes a reason to doubt that this is the form Roth's lemma needs.\n\nAdditive rather than `r·index P − r·C ≤ …` because `WithTop ℚ` has no subtraction, and `⊤`\nmust survive: a zero `P` gives `⊤` on the left and the statement stays true.\n\nOne line from `sum_index_le_index_det_pderivList_add` and `index_sepW`, because `sepW` **is**\nthat determinant — `rfl` bridges them.  That the composition is a `rfl` is the check that the\ntwo modules were built to the same shape. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_le_of_sepW_right","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_le_of_sepW_right","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L89-L99","doc":"**The height half, `y` side.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_le_of_sepW_left","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_le_of_sepW_left","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L73-L87","doc":"**The height half, `x` side:** a height bound on the separated Wronskian bounds the\n`x`-Wronskian.\n\nNeeds the `y`-Wronskian to be non-zero — which is exactly what the bounded-order criterion\n(`BoundedOrderWronskian`) is for. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.index_sepW","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.index_sepW","kind":"theorem","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L60-L71","doc":"**The index half of the induction step:** the separated Wronskian's index is exactly the\nsum of the two factors' indices.\n\nOne line from `det_separated_wronskian` and `index_separated`.  ⚠ `IsDomain` is used, and only\nby `index_separated`'s `≤` half — see that module's header for why it is not removable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sepW_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sepW_eq","kind":"theorem","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L52-L58","doc":"`det_separated_wronskian`, restated for `sepW`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.sepW","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.sepW","kind":"def","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothInductionStep.lean#L44-L50","doc":"The **separated Wronskian**: the generalized Wronskian of `∑ₖ φₖ(x)ψₖ(y)` taken with the\nmixed operator family `(Dx i) ++ (Dy j)`.  Named so the statements below fit on a page. ","signatureChunk":0}]}